 ],
 "literal": "Embracing the faded side of the small lute with a curved frame, / saying, \"Who is there that understands to resolve my suffering?\" / O bard who speaks thus, listen now with desire! / The bright yellow flower of the puncture-vine in a desolate village, / just like it faces the rising sun, / the begging bowls of impoverished poets, and his shining fame, / the lord of the great Konkaṉam region, / if you look at his chest wearing a cool garland, they will bloom indeed.",
 "translation": "O bard, who embraces the worn side of your small, curved lute, asking, 'Who is there to understand and end my misery?' Listen now with hope: Just as the bright yellow flower of the puncture-vine in a desolate village turns to face the rising sun, the empty begging bowls of impoverished poets will bloom with joy if you but look upon the broad chest, adorned with cool garlands, of the lord of the great Konkaṉam hills, whose fame shines bright.",
 "modern": "வளைந்த தண்டுடைய சிறிய யாழின் வாடிய பக்கத்தை அணைத்துக் கொண்டு, \"என் துன்பத்தைத் தீர்க்கக்கூடிய அறிவுடையவர் யார்?\" என்று கேட்கும் பாணனே, இப்போது அன்போடு கேட்பாயாக! பாழடைந்த ஊரில் உள்ள நெருஞ்சிச் செடியின் மஞ்சள் நிறப் பூவானது, மேலே எழும் சூரியனை நோக்கித் திரும்புவது போல, வறுமையுற்ற புலவர்களின் இரப்பக் கலன்களும், விளங்கும் புகழுடைய கொண்பெருங்கானத்துத் தலைவனுடைய குளிர்ந்த மாலை அணிந்த மார்பினை நோக்கினால் மகிழ்ச்சியால் மலரும்.",
 "explanation": "The poet Mōci Kīraṉār is addressing a poor bard who is wandering in deep distress, carrying his worn-out lute and wondering who will relieve his poverty. The poet guides the bard to Koṇkāṉaṅ Kiḻāṉ, the generous chieftain of the Konkaṉam region. He assures the bard that just as the yellow flower of the puncture-vine in a desolate wasteland turns to face the rising sun, the empty begging bowls of poor poets will instantly bloom with abundance upon seeing the chieftain's generous chest. The simile of the neruñci flower in a barren village facing the sun beautifully captures the transition from bleak despair to life-giving hope. The chieftain is compared to the sun, whose mere presence brings light and sustenance to those in the dark shadow of poverty.",
